	(text_box "2. Dielectric Absorption (Ceramic Capacitor Distortions)\n\nThis is where the \"special\" capacitor conversation actually matters. If you use cheap X7R or Z5U ceramic capacitors in the signal path, they change their capacitance as the voltage changes. This creates a non-linear distortion that can sound like graininess or crackling during loud bass notes.\n\nThe Fix: For any capacitor actually touching the audio signal, use Film capacitors (Polypropylene is king) or C0G/NP0 ceramics. These are stable and won't \"color\" or crackle the sound.\n\nResistors\n2. Choosing the Right Material (The \"Crackle\" Factor)\n\nNot all resistors are made the same. The material inside matters for audio:\n\nCarbon Composition (Avoid): These are the old-school brown cylinders. They are \"noisy\" and have high Voltage Coefficient of Resistance. As the audio signal voltage changes, the resistance changes slightly, which creates a grainy, crackling distortion.\n\nThick Film Surface Mount (Standard): These are okay for general electronics, but they can still be slightly noisy for sensitive audio.\n\nThin Film / Metal Film (Recommended): These are the gold standard for audio. They have very low noise and high stability. If you are using surface-mount parts, look for \"Thin Film\" resistors.\n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 288.925 28.575 0)
		(size 119.38 64.77)
		(stroke
			(width 0)
			(type dash)
		)
		(fill
			(type none)
		)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "674ab3f3-75db-4baf-8c01-95efeed74e1e")
	)
	(text "Output Impedance\nOUTPUT+ is 180 Ohms\nOUTPUT- is 250 Ohms\nDifferential is 430 Ohms"
